findings.body:
`State::mark` forgets a watch only when `gone && !p.exists()`. That check runs when the batch is processed, up to 100 ms after the first event, not when the event happened. notify's inotify backend drops its watch on DELETE, DELETE_SELF and MOVED_FROM. It does not add one back on CREATE under a NonRecursive parent.

If the directory exists again by the time the batch is processed:
- the `work` (or `git`) entry stays;
- the Create event fails `dir && !self.work.contains_key(p)`, so no walk starts;
- `apply` keeps unchanged entries without calling `paths.add`;
- activation's `set` walks only when the root moved, and for git dirs it sees `*old == new` and returns early.

Triggers:
- `rm -rf d && mkdir d`;
- a `git switch`, `checkout`, `rebase` or `stash pop` that removes and rewrites a directory;
- codegen that deletes and rewrites its output directory;
- a scripted `git worktree remove wt && git worktree add wt`, or `rm -rf r && git clone … r`. The Done note names these as cases activation brings back.

After that, edits under the path (or HEAD, index and ref writes, for git dirs) mark nothing until the current repo changes. This leaves the round-01 finding unfixed in the fast case. The new test `a_tracked_lock_file_marks_and_a_directory_made_again_is_watched_again` does the remove and the create in separate `after()` batches, so it cannot catch this.

`activated` now calls `pass(cx, false)`. That pass reloads only repos whose `Loaded` entry is 30 s old or older. The entry is set whenever any status load finishes, for any reason: startup, the end of an op, or a watcher batch on the repo's git files. The skip is safe only if the watcher would have marked every change stale, and nothing records that. Several cases depend on activation as their only refresh:
- the worktree of any non-current repo, because PLAN §7 watches only the current repo's worktree and `mark` marks other repos only on HEAD, index, refs and packed-refs;
- a Linux worktree over the 10,000-directory cap, which gets no watch at all;
- directories past `max_user_watches`;
- a failed `Watcher::new`;
- repos whose git dirs `Dirs::of` cannot resolve;
- the macOS FSEvents restart gap, and the Windows overflow or unwatch listed in TECHDEBT.

Concrete failure, on every OS: the window activates at t=0 and every status loads. The user saves a file in repo B, which is not the current repo, at t=10 and switches back at t=20. Activation sees B's status is 20 s old and skips it, so the sidebar shows B clean while it is dirty. It stays wrong until an activation more than 30 s after the load. The current repo's Changes (`fresh_for: None`, follows `On::Status`) has the same problem when its worktree is over the cap.

This breaks the goal's "activation never shows a stale status as fresh". It also contradicts §7's table ("the rescan and every status, as today") and the Done note's "Activation's rescan, which refreshes it, is unchanged".
